    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"friction\" you identify often stems from human factors rather than pure geometry; unpredictable driver behaviors like excessive merging or inattentive speed adjustments can create shockwaves that no mathematical model initially anticipated.rundabouts fail when they lack sufficient capacity for the specific turning lane dynamics at those unexpected demand spikes, which causes a ripple effect known as \"single-level bottlenecking\" into the main network. Additionally, if a roundabout becomes dominated by aggressive through-traffic, it defeats its primary purpose of smoothing flows, creating high friction and delays that signalized intersections handle more predictably as long as queues don't back up. Ultimately, while a signal can manage staged delays, a roundboat's efficiency collapses if the user behavior exceeds its robustness threshold.",
