      "content": "Traffic models do attempt to quantify these chaotic disruptions by incorporating \"stochastic fluctuation\" equations that simulate how small, random driver errors cascade into large-scale jams. While pure randomness is hard to predict, researchers often use simulation software to model how single speed changes or sudden braking can propagate backward through traffic queues, creating the \"shockwaves\" that gridlock can follow. However, until recently, models lacked the granular human variables to fully capture why specific designs fail, making these interactions a leading area for advanced agent-based modeling.",
